   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public housing law, in relation to requiring the New York\ncity housing authority to provide written notice to residents of certain\ndisruptions to water services and to establish certain guidelines regarding\nthe examination of water quality",
    "basePrintNo": "S7456",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7456-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §402-e, Pub Hous L",
    "lawSection": "Public Housing Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7456\n\nSPONSOR: KAVANAGH\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public housing law, in relation to requiring the New\nYork city housing authority to provide written notice to residents of\ncertain disruptions to water services and to establish certain guide-\nlines regarding the examination of water quality\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThis bill is intended to ensure affected New York City Housing Authority\n(NYCHA) residents receive prompt written notice when they should avoid\nthe use of water at their NYCHA development.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one requires the New York City Housing Authority (NYCHA) to\nprovide written notice to residents within 24 hours of when NYCHA has\nbeen advised by a public utility or local, state, or federal agency that\ncertain residents should avoid the use of water.\n\nSection two requires NYCHA to establish appropriate measures, proce-\ndures, and guidelines to ensure all contractors and subcontractors\ncomply with federal, state, and local laws, rules, and regulations when\ncollecting or examining water samples on behalf of NYCHA.\n\nSection three of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn summer 2022, an issue arose surrounding the possibility of arsenic in\nthe water of Jacob Riis Houses. In August, residents submitted\ncomplaints to NYCHA about the quality of their water. NYCHA contracted\nwith a company to collect water samples and test the water. The\ncontracted company subcontracted with a lab to perform the tests of the\nwater.\n\nInitial water tests revealed a presence of arsenic in the water. It was\nonly after a round of retesting by the same lab, and four days after the\ninitial results were received, that residents were notified they should\nrefrain from using the water for drinking and cooking. It was later\nrevealed that the lab performing these tests was not certified to\ncollect and test water samples in New York state.\n\nNYCHA contracted with a new company to perform additional testing of the\nwater. The results from the new lab indicated that there was no arsenic\nin the water and the water was safe to consume. While ultimately there\nwas never a danger to the health of residents this time, NYCHA must\nprovide better oversight of their contractors and subcontractors to\nbetter protect residents.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
